<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Rajgarh State</h1><p class="st-meta">1640 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Rajgarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Umat Parmar Rajput state of Malwa, sister state of Narsinghgarh, ruled from Rajgarh on the Newaj river.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Rajgarh was ruled by the Umat clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmar-rajputs">Parmar Rajputs</a>, settled in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> known after them as Umatwara. The capital was established at Rajgarh around 1640. In 1681 the estate was divided and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/narsinghgarh-state">Narsinghgarh State</a> formed from part of it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers, styled Rawat and later Raja, governed a rolling plateau of wheat and opium farming. In the 18th century they paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scindias">Sindhia</a>, before British paramountcy in 1818. Rajgarh was an eleven-gun salute state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhopal-agency">Bhopal Agency</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rajgarh joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madhya-bharat">Madhya Bharat</a> in 1948 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956. It is now a district headquarters.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rajgarh-state.jpg" alt="Rajgarh State" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Andreas Birken · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajgarhflag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1640 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rajgarh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malvi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rajgarh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1794&amp;focus=rajgarh-state" aria-label="Rajgarh State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6723 1293 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rajgarh State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7043 1549L7364 1601M7043 1549L7364 1602M7043 1549L7064 1471"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7043 1549h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7043 1549h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rajgarh. <a href="/atlas?year=1794&amp;focus=rajgarh-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
